+---
+
+# Change Store Design
+
+## Context and Design Philosophy
+
+The change store is the authoritative source of event board revisions. It owns the revision history, read indexes, and the publication outbox that feeds the delivery component. Every committed edit from the command handler appends exactly one revision to the event's history. The store is designed for the self-hosted WebSocket delivery approach: it must support replay from any revision, cursor-based queries for catch-up, and a reliable outbox for delivery to publish new revisions to connected WebSocket sessions.
+
+## Revision Model
+
+Each event maintains an ordered sequence of revisions:
+
+- `revision_id`: monotonically increasing integer per event (1, 2, 3...)
+- `event_id`: the event this revision belongs to
+- `actor_id`: the authorized editor who submitted the command
+- `timestamp`: server wall-clock time of commitment (UTC)
+- `operations`: array of atomic board mutations (stall status changes, notes, etc.)
+- `previous_hash`: hash of the prior revision for tamper evidence (optional, can be added later)
+
+Revisions are immutable once committed. The latest revision represents the current authoritative board state.
+
+## Read Indexes
+
+Two read paths serve different consumers:
+
+| Index | Consumer | Query Pattern |
+|-------|----------|---------------|
+| Latest revision per event | Browsers on initial load, editors submitting commands | `SELECT MAX(revision_id) FROM revisions WHERE event_id = ?` |
+| Cursor query (revision range) | Browsers catching up after disconnect | `SELECT * FROM revisions WHERE event_id = ? AND revision_id > ? ORDER BY revision_id ASC` |
+
+The cursor query is the primary read path for browser catch-up. It must return revisions in order, with no gaps, up to a configurable batch limit (default 100 revisions per response).
+
+## Publication Outbox
+
+The outbox enables reliable publication to the delivery component without dual-write risk. When a command handler commits a revision, it writes the new revision AND an outbox entry in the same transaction:
+
+- `outbox_id`: monotonically increasing global ID
+- `event_id`: the event that changed
+- `revision_id`: the new revision number
+- `created_at`: timestamp of outbox entry creation
+- `published_at`: nullable timestamp when delivery acknowledged publication
+
+Delivery reads the outbox in `outbox_id` order, publishes to relevant WebSocket sessions, and marks entries published. Unpublished entries are retried on delivery restart.
+
+## Retention
+
+- **Active events** (event hours published): retain all revisions
+- **Completed events** (after event hours): retain 7 days of revisions from event close, then archive
+- **Outbox entries**: retain until published + 24 hours, then GC
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Realtime delivery approach | Self-hosted WebSockets | 5s HTTPS polling, hosted realtime service | HLD decision: avoids vendor bill and coupling; accepts ops burden for subsecond updates. See `docs/decisions/realtime-delivery-approach.md` |
+| Revision ID scope | Per-event monotonically increasing | Global UUID, global sequence | Per-event sequence enables efficient cursor queries and natural ordering for catch-up; global IDs add no value for this domain |
+| Outbox pattern | Transactional outbox in same DB | Separate message queue (Redis, Kafka) | Avoids dual-write, no additional infrastructure, sufficient for 800 browsers and 20 edits/sec bursts |
+| Cursor query batch limit | 100 revisions per response | 50, 200, unbounded | 100 balances round-trips for 20-min disconnect catch-up (~2400 revisions max) against response size |
+| Revision hash chaining | Deferred (optional) | Include from start | Tamper evidence is a security hardening; not required for pilot availability goals |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Self-hosted WebSockets chosen as delivery approach — change store must support replayable revisions and outbox for delivery
+
+### Deferred
+1. Whether to add `previous_hash` chaining for tamper evidence (security hardening)
+2. Exact archive format and storage for 7-day retention after event close
+3. Whether outbox GC runs as a background job or on delivery acknowledgment
+
+## References
+
+- High-level design: `docs/high-level-design.md`
+- Delivery design: `docs/intent/delivery/delivery-design.md`
+- Browser sync design: `docs/intent/browser-sync/browser-sync-design.md`
+- EARS syntax reference: `references/ears-syntax.md`
\ No newline at end of file
