+---
+parent: high-level-design
+prefix: DELIVERY
+---
+
+# Delivery Design
+
+## Context and Design Philosophy
+
+The delivery component manages self-hosted WebSocket sessions for all connected browsers. It reads from the change store's publication outbox, fans out new revisions to subscribed sessions, and handles connection lifecycle: accept, authenticate, subscribe, heartbeat, reconnect, and graceful close. The design accepts the operational burden of connection management to achieve subsecond update latency without a hosted realtime vendor.
+
+## Session Model
+
+Each browser opens one WebSocket connection per event it views. A session carries:
+
+- `session_id`: opaque UUID assigned on connection accept
+- `event_id`: the event this session subscribes to
+- `actor_id`: the authenticated coordinator (viewer or editor)
+- `last_ack_revision`: highest revision the browser has acknowledged (for catch-up)
+- `connected_at`: timestamp of connection establishment
+- `last_heartbeat`: timestamp of last received ping/pong
+
+Sessions are tracked in an in-memory registry with optional Redis backing for multi-instance horizontal scaling (pilot runs single-instance).
+
+## Protocol
+
+### Client → Server Messages
+
+| Message Type | Payload | Purpose |
+|--------------|---------|---------|
+| `subscribe` | `{ event_id, last_revision? }` | Join event, optional catch-up from revision |
+| `ping` | `{ timestamp }` | Heartbeat, measure RTT |
+| `ack` | `{ revision_id }` | Confirm receipt of revision (enables server GC of sent buffer) |
+
+### Server → Client Messages
+
+| Message Type | Payload | Purpose |
+|--------------|---------|---------|
+| `welcome` | `{ session_id, event_id, current_revision, board_snapshot }` | Initial state on connect/subscribe |
+| `revision` | `{ revision_id, operations, timestamp, actor_id }` | New committed revision |
+| `catchup` | `{ revisions: [...] }` | Batch of revisions for catch-up |
+| `pong` | `{ timestamp, server_time }` | Heartbeat response |
+| `error` | `{ code, message, retryable }` | Protocol or auth error |
+
+### Message Flow
+
+1. Browser connects → server accepts, authenticates via session cookie/token
+2. Browser sends `subscribe` with `event_id` and optional `last_revision`
+3. If `last_revision` provided and < current: server sends `catchup` batch from change store cursor query
+4. Server sends `welcome` with current board snapshot and `current_revision`
+5. On each new revision from outbox: server fans out `revision` to all subscribed sessions
+6. Browser replies `ack` per revision (or batched)
+7. Heartbeat: server sends `ping` every 15s; client must respond `pong` within 10s or connection closes
+8. On disconnect: session removed from registry; on reconnect, browser repeats from step 1 with `last_revision = last_ack_revision`
+
+## Outbox Consumption
+
+Delivery runs a background consumer that:
+
+1. Polls change store outbox for unpublished entries (by `outbox_id` cursor)
+2. For each entry, loads the revision from change store
+3. Fans out `revision` message to all sessions subscribed to that `event_id`
+4. Marks outbox entry published (with `published_at`) on successful fan-out to all sessions
+5. On fan-out failure to any session: retries with exponential backoff; session marked for reconnect if persistent
+
+Fan-out is best-effort per session: a slow session doesn't block others. Unacked revisions are buffered per session up to a limit (default 50); excess triggers catch-up on next reconnect.
+
+## Connection Capacity
+
+- Target: 800 simultaneous browsers (1 connection each)
+- Single-instance pilot: Node.js event loop handles 800 WebSockets comfortably
+- Horizontal scaling: add Redis-backed session registry and outbox consumer coordination when needed
+- Opening-time burst: 20 edits/sec → 20 outbox entries/sec → 20 fan-outs/sec to up to 800 sessions each = 16K messages/sec peak, within single-instance capacity
+
+## Reconnect & Catch-Up
+
+Browser reconnect logic is owned by the browser-sync component, but delivery supports it via:
+
+- `subscribe` message accepts `last_revision` for cursor-based catch-up
+- Change store cursor query returns ordered revisions from `last_revision + 1`
+- Session registry survives delivery restart (in-memory for pilot; Redis for scale)
+- On delivery restart: unacked outbox entries re-published; sessions re-authenticate on reconnect
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Realtime delivery approach | Self-hosted WebSockets | 5s HTTPS polling, hosted realtime service | HLD decision: avoids vendor bill and coupling; accepts ops burden for subsecond updates. See `docs/decisions/realtime-delivery-approach.md` |
+| Protocol format | JSON over WebSocket | MessagePack, protobuf | JSON is debuggable, sufficient for payload sizes (~1KB/revision), no schema registry needed |
+| Heartbeat interval | 15s ping, 10s pong timeout | 30s/15s, 10s/5s | 15s balances detection speed against mobile network variance; 10s timeout avoids premature closes on slow networks |
+| Session registry | In-memory (pilot) | Redis from start | Pilot is single-instance; Redis adds complexity not needed until horizontal scaling |
+| Acknowledgment | Per-revision `ack` from browser | Implicit (TCP ACK), batched | Explicit ack enables server to GC sent buffer and detect stalled sessions; batched ack adds complexity |
+| Outbox consumer | Polling (100ms interval) | Long-poll, push notification | 100ms poll is simple, adds ~50ms avg latency, acceptable for subsecond target |
+| Max buffered unacked revisions | 50 per session | 20, 100, unbounded | 50 covers ~2.5s at 20 edits/sec burst; larger buffers increase memory per session |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Self-hosted WebSockets chosen — delivery owns session management, outbox consumption, fan-out
+
+### Deferred
+1. Redis-backed session registry for horizontal scaling (post-pilot)
+2. Whether to compress `revision` messages for high-frequency bursts
+3. Exact backoff schedule for failed fan-out retries
+4. Whether to add per-session message sequencing for duplicate detection
+
+## References
+
+- High-level design: `docs/high-level-design.md`
+- Change store design: `docs/intent/change-store/change-store-design.md`
+- Browser sync design: `docs/intent/browser-sync/browser-sync-design.md`
+- EARS syntax reference: `references/ears-syntax.md`
